# Patch files are BYTES, not text. Do not normalise them in either direction.
#
# A unified diff's context lines must match the target file byte for byte, and FreeCAD
# 1.1.3 ships a mix: 33 of the 80 files this port touches are CRLF upstream, 47 are LF.
# So patches/freecad.patch legitimately contains both, and any normalisation corrupts it.
#
# This was `text eol=lf` for exactly one commit, which is the tidy-looking choice and the
# wrong one: it would have rewritten those 1,844 CRLF context lines to LF and reproduced
# the failure it was meant to prevent -- GNU patch 2.8 rejects a mismatched hunk outright
# with "different line endings", while the msys patch(1) used to generate the file
# tolerates it silently.
*.patch -text

# Shell scripts run on the Linux build/test servers — force LF and keep them executable.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Docker reads .dockerignore line by line; a CRLF checkout leaves a trailing carriage
# return on every pattern, so "!play-gui" silently stops matching and the build context
# goes back to shipping tens of GB. Compose is less fussy but is kept consistent.
.dockerignore text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml text eol=lf
